# Methods

## Abstract {#abstract}

NASA's SPHEREx telescope photographs the whole infrared sky again and again, but each photograph of a given spot is taken in a slightly different colour. Because of this, simply flicking between pictures makes ordinary stars appear to change. Epoch plays all [[meta.n_frames|int]] public pictures of the first data release in time order and compares each picture with the previous picture taken by the same detector, which removes most of these false changes. To reach objects too faint for one picture, it adds up many pictures along a known path. This page sets out every method we used: the mathematics, how each method was tested on data it was not tuned on, what it measured, and which methods we tried and did not keep.

## 1. Data {#data}

All measurements use the Level-2 spectral images of SPHEREx Data Release 1 (DR1; doi:10.26131/IRSA678), read pixel by pixel from NASA's public archive copy. SPHEREx (Bock et al. 2026) images the sky with six 2040 × 2040-pixel detectors at 6.15″ per pixel. Detectors 1–3 cover 0.75–2.42 µm and detectors 4–6 cover 2.42–5.0 µm, and the pairs 1–4, 2–5 and 3–6 are exposed simultaneously. Each detector sits behind a linear variable filter, so the wavelength it records changes smoothly across its face. A source lands at a different wavelength on every visit and builds up a 102-channel spectrum over several visits, at a resolving power of about 41 on detectors 1–3, 35 on detector 4, and 110 and 130 on detectors 5 and 6.

**Wavelength map.** For each detector the archive's spectral coordinate table gives the central wavelength $\lambda_c(x, y)$ and bandwidth $\Delta\lambda(x, y)$ on a grid of nodes; we interpolate bilinearly between nodes. In DR1 the table is the same for every picture of a detector.

![Central wavelength across the six detectors, with one star's positions](figs/v2/wavelength_map.svg "Central wavelength across detectors 1–6 (a–f), interpolated from the archive's spectral coordinate tables, with the position of one constant star near the north ecliptic pole in each of its [[e18bs.n_pictures|int]] pictures (white points). Each visit places the star at a different point along the wavelength gradient, so its spectrum builds up over the year.")

**Archive, index and flags.** Each Level-2 file holds a calibrated image in MJy sr⁻¹, its variance, a quality-flag plane, a zodiacal-light model, the effective PSF and a TAN-SIP coordinate solution. Files are read by HTTPS range requests, so a small cutout costs kilobytes rather than the whole file. Our index of the release, built from the bucket listing and a range read of every header, holds [[meta.n_frames|int]] pictures taken between [[meta.mjd_min|date]] and [[meta.mjd_max|date]], each with detector, time, footprint and quality assessment. Photometry masks the flags SUR_ERROR, NONFUNC, MISSING_DATA, HOT, COLD, NONLINEAR, PERSIST, BLOOM, SNOWBALL, HALO and SATELLITE_HALO (the photometry mask of Akeson et al. 2026), plus OVERFLOW for sources that may saturate. OUTLIER and TRANSIENT are kept, because the pipeline sets them on pixels that changed between exposures, which includes moving and variable sources.

**Effective PSF.** The archive's effective PSF, ePSF (Anderson & King 2000), is the optical PSF convolved with the pixel response, tabulated at five times the native sampling for zones across each detector. Its value at an offset is the fraction of a source's flux falling in a native pixel at that offset, so a model image needs only interpolation. We use the zone nearest the source, interpolate with bicubic splines, and keep the 7 × 7 native pixels around the source, renormalised to unit sum. *Simplification:* fluxes are therefore the flux inside this box, without an aperture correction for the outer wings. The missing fraction is a few per cent and the same in every picture of a zone, so it cancels in ratios and light curves but not in absolute comparisons. On the same pixels of five isolated, non-variable stars, our fluxes are [[e31s.stars_ours_over_irsa_D4|f2]]–[[e31s.stars_ours_over_irsa_D1|f2]] times those of IRSA's published SPHEREx aperture-photometry recipe, depending on detector, as this missing wing predicts.

**Picture model.** Within a cutout, the data are modelled as point sources on a planar background:

$$
d(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{k} A_k\,\mathrm{ePSF}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}_k) + b(\mathbf{x}),
\qquad
b(\mathbf{x}) = c_0 + c_1\,(x-\bar{x}) + c_2\,(y-\bar{y}).
$$

The positions $\mathbf{x}_k$ are fixed: every Gaia DR3 star brighter than $G = 19$–$19.5$, moved with its proper motion to the picture's time, plus the target at its predicted position. Only the amplitudes are free. With design matrix $X$, parameters $\boldsymbol{\beta} = (A_1, \dots, A_K, c_0, c_1, c_2)$ and $W = \operatorname{diag}(1/\sigma_p^2)$ from the archive variance, the weighted least-squares solution and its covariance are

$$
\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}} = \left(X^{\mathsf T} W X\right)^{-1} X^{\mathsf T} W\,\mathbf{d},
\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}\left(\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\right) = \left(X^{\mathsf T} W X\right)^{-1}.
$$

Masked pixels are excluded, and the system is solved by singular-value decomposition of $W^{1/2}X$, discarding singular values below $10^{-12}$ of the largest, so coincident sources give a large, correctly reported error rather than a numerical failure. Flux density is $F = A\,\Omega$, with $\Omega$ the pixel solid angle. Fit quality near the source is a local $\chi^2$ within 2 pixels, with $n_\mathrm{local} - \sum_i h_{ii}$ degrees of freedom, where $h_{ii}$ are the leverages.

**Error model.** Repeated measurements scatter more than the archive variance implies. For photometry of many sources each formal error is rescaled with two numbers per detector $d$, measured on stars not known to vary:

$$
\sigma_i^2 = \left(s_d\,\sigma_{\mathrm{formal},i}\right)^2 + \left(f_d\,M_i\right)^2 ,
$$

where $M_i$ is the source's model flux in picture $i$. $s_d$ makes the median reduced $\chi^2$ of the static model (Section 7) equal 1 for constant stars of $15 \le G < 17$ with $f_d = 0$; $f_d$ then does the same for constant stars brighter than $G = 13$. At the north ecliptic pole:

| Detector | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| $s_d$ | [[e18b.gate0.s_d.1|f2]] | [[e18b.gate0.s_d.2|f2]] | [[e18b.gate0.s_d.3|f2]] | [[e18b.gate0.s_d.4|f2]] | [[e18b.gate0.s_d.5|f2]] | [[e18b.gate0.s_d.6|f2]] |
| $f_d$ | [[e18b.gate0.f_d.1|pct]] | [[e18b.gate0.f_d.2|pct]] | [[e18b.gate0.f_d.3|pct]] | [[e18b.gate0.f_d.4|pct]] | [[e18b.gate0.f_d.5|pct]] | [[e18b.gate0.f_d.6|pct]] |

Wherever a result depends on the size of an error, that error is also measured empirically, on comparison stars or empty positions in the same pictures, and the larger value is used.

## 2. Change maps {#change}

A change map marks the pixels of one picture that differ from what the other pictures of the same position predict. We compared several ways of building that prediction, all on the same grid and with the same noise model.

**Grid and noise.** Each picture is resampled bilinearly onto a north-up 6.15″ grid of 48 × 48 pixels around the target; a grid pixel is blank if any of its four native neighbours is masked. From each picture $v_e$ we subtract a 3σ-clipped median background and measure $\sigma_e = 1.4826\,\operatorname{MAD}$. Because the scene is undersampled and seen at a different wavelength and sub-pixel phase in each picture, residuals at stars exceed the pixel noise, so every map uses

$$
\sigma_p^2 = \sigma_e^2 + \left(f\,S_p\right)^2 ,
$$

with $S_p$ the reference value at pixel $p$ and $f = $ [[f1_calibration.f|f3]], set on a calibration field so that the median of $(v-S)^2/\sigma_p^2$ within one pixel of Gaia stars with $G < 16$ is 1. *Simplification:* for Gaussian residuals that median would be 0.455, which would give a larger floor ([[f1_calibration.note_gaussian_median.f|f2]]); since the thresholds below are set empirically on these maps, the choice moves the thresholds, not the false-alarm rate. The floor also means that a star changing by less than about [[f1_calibration.f|pct]] of its brightness cannot produce a significant pixel: change maps cannot show the variability of bright stars, which Section 7 measures by photometry.

**The references.** For picture $e$ of detector $d(e)$, with background-subtracted value $v_e(p)$:

*Plain blink (M1)* compares with the previous picture in time, $q$, of any detector:

$$
z_e(p) = \frac{v_e(p) - v_q(p)}{\sqrt{\sigma_e^2 + \sigma_q^2 + \left(f\,\lvert v_q(p)\rvert\right)^2}} .
$$

*One detector at a time (M2)* uses the same statistic with $q$ the previous picture from the same detector, which is close in wavelength, so the stellar spectrum largely cancels.

*Median reference (M3)* uses $R_e(p) = \operatorname{median}_{o \in \mathcal{D}(e) \setminus \{e\}} v_o(p)$ over the other pictures of the same detector. Up to twice, the single picture deviating most at a pixel is dropped if it lies beyond $4\sigma$:

$$
z_e(p) = \frac{v_e(p) - R_e(p)}{\sqrt{\sigma_e^2 + \left(f\,R_e(p)\right)^2}} .
$$

*Median reference with per-pixel scale (M3b)* divides M3's map by a scale measured on the other pictures $o$ that M3 kept:

$$
z^{\mathrm{b}}_e(p) = \frac{z_e(p)}{c_e(p)},
\qquad
c_e(p) = \max\left(1,\; 1.4826\,\operatorname{median}_{o}\left\lvert \frac{v_o(p) - R_e(p)}{\sqrt{\sigma_o^2 + \left(f R_e(p)\right)^2}} \right\rvert\right),
$$

with $c_e = 1$ where fewer than five such pictures exist. Picture $e$ never enters its own scale, so a source present only in $e$ cannot suppress itself. The scale is large where a bright star's residual changes from picture to picture.

*Wavelength-aware fit (M4)* fits, at each pixel and detector, a straight line in wavelength by weighted least squares:

$$
m_e(p) = a_d(p) + b_d(p)\left[\lambda_e(p) - \bar{\lambda}_d(p)\right],
\qquad
z_e(p) = \frac{v_e(p) - m_e(p)}{\sqrt{\sigma_e^2 + \left(f\,m_e(p)\right)^2}} ,
$$

with the slope set to zero below four pictures and the same rejection. *Simplification:* unlike M3, picture $e$ is part of its own fit unless rejected, so a faint source slightly lowers its own residual. An independently proposed baseline (M5) takes the median of the 15 same-detector pictures nearest in wavelength, leaving $e$ out.

**Spikes, components and thresholds.** Unflagged single hot pixels, up to thousands of σ, survive in every map. Every map therefore gets the same spike rule: a pixel with $\lvert z\rvert > 5$ is zeroed if the median $\lvert z\rvert$ of its 3 × 3 neighbourhood is below 2.5. A change is an 8-connected group of at least two pixels with $z > k$; only positive groups count, since a source that appears is positive in its own picture's map. For blinks the noise term uses the reference picture's value; using the larger of the two would put the source's own flux in the denominator and cap its significance near $1/f$. For each method, $k$ is the smallest value on a 0.05 grid at which calibration fields with nothing added give at most the target rate of false changes per picture. It is then applied unchanged to test fields. At 0.01 per picture on the first calibration field, $k$ = [[e1_thresholds.methods.M1.k|f2]] (M1), [[e1_thresholds.methods.M2.k|f2]] (M2), [[e1_thresholds.methods.M3.k|f1]] (M3) and [[e1_thresholds.methods.M4.k|f1]] (M4).

**Injection–recovery.** One point source per picture is planted in the raw native cutout with the picture's own ePSF zone, before resampling, so it passes through the same interpolation as real sources. Its flux is set by the signal-to-noise a PSF fit at the known position would reach,

$$
\left(\frac{S}{N}\right)_\mathrm{frame} = \frac{F\,\sqrt{\sum_p P_p^2}}{\sigma_e},
$$

with $P_p$ the unit-sum ePSF, at levels 3, 5, 8, 12 and 20. Positions are uniform in the central 40 × 40 pixels, at least 3 pixels from Gaia stars brighter than $G = 19$, with fixed seeds. A source is recovered if a change above $k$ lies within 1.5 pixels. Intervals come from 2,000 bootstrap resamples of fields.

**Twenty random fields.** Fields were drawn uniformly on the sky by a fixed seed, 10 at Galactic latitude $\lvert b\rvert > 30°$, 5 at 15–30° and 5 below 15°. Viewing one detector at a time removes a median [[headline.number]]% (95% interval [[headline.err_low]]–[[headline.err_high]]%) of the changes the plain blink flags, over [[e2.summary.ratio_corr_over_naive_all.n_fields]] fields and [[e2.total_frames|int]] pictures. These counts use groups with $\lvert z\rvert > 5$ of either sign, without the spike rule, and for blinks the larger of the two pictures' values in the noise term. This is a statement about false changes only.

![Changes per field](figs/v2/fig_e2.svg "Changes flagged in each of 20 random fields by the plain blink (crosses), one detector at a time (circles) and the wavelength-aware fit (triangles), ordered by Galactic latitude. Counts are summed over all pictures of a field; the vertical axis is logarithmic.")

**The wavelength-aware fit does not find fainter changes.** The test required the wavelength-aware fit to reach 50% recovery at lower signal-to-noise than every other method, with a bootstrap interval on the difference excluding zero. No method reached 50% at any level, so the criterion was not met. At S/N 20 the wavelength-aware fit recovered [[e1.per_method.M4.efficiency.4|pct]], one detector at a time [[e1.per_method.M2.efficiency.4|pct]] and the median reference [[e1.per_method.M3.efficiency.4|pct]]. The lead of the wavelength-aware fit comes from its threshold. On test fields its false-alarm rate was [[e1_test_false_rates.per_method.M4.rate_per_frame|f3]] per picture against [[e1_test_false_rates.per_method.M3.rate_per_frame|f3]] for the median reference, so one calibration field did not hold the methods to equal rates. At a common threshold $k = 5$, recovery at S/N 20 is [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M3.efficiency.4|pct]] for the median reference, [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M5.efficiency.4|pct]] for M5, [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M4.efficiency.4|pct]] for the wavelength-aware fit, [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M2.efficiency.4|pct]] for one detector at a time and [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M1.efficiency.4|pct]] for the plain blink. At that threshold the false-alarm rates differ: the plain blink flags [[e2_d1.counts.M1.total|int]] changes over the 20 fields, one detector at a time [[e2_d1.counts.M2.total|int]].

![Recovery of planted sources](figs/v2/fig_e1.svg "Fraction of planted point sources recovered against their single-picture signal-to-noise, for each change map at its calibration-field threshold. Bands are 95% field-bootstrap intervals.")

**Median reference against one detector at a time.** M3 and M3b were then compared with M2 on 20 new test fields and 10 ecliptic fields, each at least 0.5° from every earlier field, with thresholds pooled over eight new calibration fields at 0.01, 0.03 and 0.10 false changes per picture. A challenger beats M2 if, at two or more rates, its S/N-20 recovery exceeds M2's with the 95% interval of the paired difference excluding zero, and its realised test false-alarm rate is at most 1.5 times the target.

The test used [[e27s.n_test_fields]] fields ([[e27s.test_frames]] pictures). At S/N 20 and 0.01, 0.03 and 0.10 false changes per picture, M3b recovers [[e27s.recall20_M3B_r1|f2]], [[e27s.recall20_M3B_r3|f2]] and [[e27s.recall20_M3B_r10|f2]], against [[e27s.recall20_M2_r1|f2]], [[e27s.recall20_M2_r3|f2]] and [[e27s.recall20_M2_r10|f2]] for M2. The paired difference at 0.03 is [[e27s.diff20_M3B_r3|f2]] [[[e27s.diff20_M3B_r3_lo|f2]], [[e27s.diff20_M3B_r3_hi|f2]]], and M3b's realised false-alarm rates ([[e27s.fa_M3B_r1|f3]], [[e27s.fa_M3B_r3|f3]], [[e27s.fa_M3B_r10|f3]]) are within 1.5 times the targets, so M3b beats M2 at all three rates. M3 recovers more than M2 too, but its realised false-alarm rate on the test fields exceeds 1.5 times the target at every rate ([[e27s.fa_M3_r3|f3]] at 0.03), so by the rule it ties. The leave-one-out scale $c$ is what keeps M3b's thresholds transferable between fields. M2's own realised rate also overshoots ([[e27s.fa_M2_r3|f3]] at 0.03), which favours M2. Two challengers were tested, so the single "beats" carries a mild multiple-comparison caveat. At S/N 3–5 no method recovers more than about 2%. The asteroid secondary is inconclusive: the 10 ecliptic fields hold [[e27s.n_sightings]] catalogued sightings of one asteroid brighter than V = 19, and no method detects it. On real asteroids (Section 3, test 1), at the same thresholds, M3b marks [[e34s.ast_M3BK_hits_V17p5]] of [[e34s.ast_n_V17p5]] sightings and M2 [[e34s.ast_M2K_hits_V17p5]].

![Median reference against one detector at a time](figs/v2/e27_recall.svg "Fraction of planted sources recovered on the [[e27s.n_test_fields]] test fields against their S/N per picture, at 0.03 false changes per picture, for the median of the other pictures from the same detector with (M3b) and without (M3) the leave-one-out scale, and for the previous picture from the same detector (M2). Shaded bands are 95% intervals.")

## 3. Learned change detector {#detector}

The learned detector ranks candidate changes by how much they resemble a point source that appeared, rather than a bright-star residual or an artefact. It follows the two-stage real–bogus design of optical transient surveys (Goldstein et al. 2015; Duev et al. 2019; Acero-Cuellar et al. 2023), with a reference built from SPHEREx's own pictures.

**Candidates.** Stage 1 takes the positive local maxima with $z^\mathrm{b} \ge 3$ of the M3b map, whose reference never contains the picture itself.

**Network.** Stage 2 scores a 9 × 9-pixel cutout around each candidate with five channels — picture/$\sigma_e$, reference/$\sigma_e$, $z^\mathrm{b}$, the scale $c_e$, and a valid-pixel mask — plus a one-hot detector vector $\mathbf{u}$. All inputs are in noise units, with no absolute flux scale: an earlier linker built on absolute features did not transfer between fields.

| Layer | Operation | Output | Parameters |
|---|---|---|---|
| Input | cutout | 9 × 9 × 5 | — |
| Conv 1 | 3 × 3 valid, 5 → 12 | 7 × 7 × 12 | 552 |
| Conv 2 | 3 × 3 valid, 12 → 16 | 5 × 5 × 16 | 1,744 |
| Conv 3 | 3 × 3 valid, 16 → 16 | 3 × 3 × 16 | 2,320 |
| Concatenate | flatten, append $\mathbf{u}$ | 150 | — |
| Dense 1 | 150 → 16 | 16 | 2,416 |
| Dense 2 | 16 → 1 | logit $s$ | 17 |
| Total | | | 7,049 |

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{h}_l &= \phi\left(W_l \ast \mathbf{h}_{l-1} + \mathbf{b}_l\right),\quad l = 1, 2, 3,\quad \mathbf{h}_0 = \mathbf{x},\\
s &= \mathbf{w}_5^{\mathsf T}\,\phi\left(W_4\left[\operatorname{vec}(\mathbf{h}_3);\,\mathbf{u}\right] + \mathbf{b}_4\right) + b_5,
\qquad
\hat{y} = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-s}} ,
\end{aligned}
$$

with $\ast$ a valid convolution and $\phi$ a pointwise nonlinearity.

Here $\phi(x) = \max(0, x)$ (ReLU). Weights are initialised He-normal and biases at zero. The convolutions are 5→12→16→16 channels on the 9 × 9 cutout; the flattened 144 values plus the six-way detector one-hot $\mathbf{u}$ feed a dense layer of 16 units. The network has 7,049 parameters. The input channels are $g(v/\sigma)$, $g(m/\sigma)$, $g(z)$, $\ln c$ and a validity mask, with $g(x) = \operatorname{sign}(x)\ln(1 + |x|)$, so that a single bad pixel at thousands of σ cannot saturate the input.

**Loss and training.** The binary cross-entropy

$$
\mathcal{L} = -\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\left[\,y_n \ln \hat{y}_n + \left(1 - y_n\right)\ln\left(1 - \hat{y}_n\right)\right]
$$

is minimised with Adam in JAX, under exactly the browser tool's preprocessing, so the weights run unchanged in the browser. The pool is 21 fields, split by field into 15 for training and 6 for validation. Positives ($y = 1$, [[e30.training.n_pos]]) are sources injected with the ePSF at S/N log-uniform in 3–30, 12 passes per field. Negatives ([[e30.training.n_neg]]) are every candidate from passes with nothing injected: the real false candidates of stage 1. Augmentation uses the eight flips and rotations, random subsets of 50–100% of each field's pictures (which change the reference), and ±10% noise jitter. Adam (learning rate $2\times10^{-3}$ with cosine decay, batch 512, decoupled weight decay $10^{-4}$) is run for two seeds, with early stopping on validation recovery at 0.03 false candidates per picture. A six-feature logistic regression is the ablation, used instead if the network does not beat it on validation.

**Thresholds and test.** For $r \in \{0.01, 0.03, 0.10\}$ false candidates per picture, $t_r$ is the smallest score at which 12 new calibration fields, with nothing injected, give at most $r$; 0.03 is the tool's operating point. The thresholds and weights, whose hash is recorded, are applied unchanged to 20 new test fields (and, as a secondary, 10 ecliptic fields) at least 0.5° from all earlier fields. The primary statistic is recovery averaged over S/N 3, 5, 8, 12 and 20, paired against M3b and M2, with 2,000 field-bootstrap resamples. The detector beats a baseline only if both 95% intervals exclude zero and the realised false-alarm rate is at most 1.5 times the target, at two or more rates. Otherwise the tool ranks candidates by M3b. Secondary measures cover matched realised rates, 20 × 20-pixel crops, Galactic latitude, detector, and catalogued asteroids brighter than $V = 19$.

On validation the network recovers [[e30s.val_cnn|f2]] (mean over the five S/N levels at 0.03 false candidates per picture). The logistic regression recovers [[e30s.val_lr|f3]] and the stage-1 statistic alone [[e30s.val_z|raw]], so the network ships. On the [[e30s.n_test_fields]] test fields ([[e30s.test_pictures]] pictures) it recovers [[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r1|f3]] [[[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r1_lo|f3]], [[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r1_hi|f3]]], [[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r3|f3]] [[[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r3_lo|f3]], [[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r3_hi|f3]]] and [[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r10|f3]] [[[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r10_lo|f3]], [[e30s.mean_recall_CNN_r10_hi|f3]]] at 0.01, 0.03 and 0.10. The realised false-candidate rates are [[e30s.fa_CNN_r1|f3]], [[e30s.fa_CNN_r3|f3]] and [[e30s.fa_CNN_r10|f3]] per picture. By S/N at 0.03, recovery is [[e30s.recall3_CNN_r3|f3]], [[e30s.recall5_CNN_r3|f2]], [[e30s.recall8_CNN_r3|f2]], [[e30s.recall12_CNN_r3|f2]] and [[e30s.recall20_CNN_r3|f2]] at S/N 3, 5, 8, 12 and 20. M3b and M2 without flags recover at most 0.001 at every rate. Their calibrated thresholds are thousands of σ ([[e30s.thr_M3B_r3|f0]] for M3b at 0.03) because unmasked bad pixels dominate their tails, so by the test's rule the network beats both at all three rates. That comparison is lopsided, so we also report a reference line that was not part of the rule: M3b with the archive's flags, as in the median-reference comparison, recovers [[e30s.mean_recall_M3BF_r3|f3]] [[[e30s.mean_recall_M3BF_r3_lo|f3]], [[e30s.mean_recall_M3BF_r3_hi|f3]]] at 0.03. The network, without flags, is [[e30.verdict.CNN.r3.diff_vs_M3BF|f3]] [[[e30.verdict.CNN.r3.diff_vs_M3BF_ci95.0|f3]], [[e30.verdict.CNN.r3.diff_vs_M3BF_ci95.1|f3]]] above it. Removing the [[e30s.n_shared_test_pictures]] test pictures that also appear in training or calibration fields changes nothing. Two limits apply. On 20 × 20-pixel crops, the default 2′ view, the network recovers [[e30s.crop_mean_recall_CNN_r3|f2]] against [[e30s.crop_mean_recall_M3BF_r3|f2]] for flagged M3b, so it brings no gain there. And on 21 earlier ecliptic fields, real catalogued asteroids with $z$ 3–5 score below injected sources of the same $z$ (0 of 11 above threshold, against 25%; KS $p = 0.004$): the simulated sources are easier than real faint movers. On the [[e30s.n_sightings]] catalogued sightings ($V < 19$) of [[e30s.n_objects]] asteroids in the 10 ecliptic fields, the network detects none at any rate. M3b with flags detects [[e30s.ast_M3BF_r3_hits]], all of one asteroid. The gain is measured on injected point sources and does not yet carry over to real moving objects. In the browser, detection on 250 pictures takes about 0.16 s at 20 pixels and 2.5 s at 78 pixels.

![Recovery of injected sources by the learned detector and the baselines](figs/v2/e30_recall.svg "Fraction of injected sources recovered on the [[e30s.n_test_fields]] test fields against their S/N per picture, at 0.03 false candidates per picture, for the network (no quality flags), the median reference with the archive's flags (a reference, not part of the test rule), and the median reference and one-detector comparison without flags. Shaded bands are 95% intervals.")

**Version 2 and tests on real objects.** The first network rejected bright real sources, because during training anything brighter than S/N 30 was a bad pixel: it marked none of the 12 sightings of (196) Philomela (V 12.7). Version 2 keeps the architecture, loss and optimiser and adds two kinds of positives: injected sources at S/N 30–3000, and real stars transplanted by shifting one picture by 6–12 pixels against its own leave-one-out reference (a positive if the star's new position is blank in the reference). Thresholds were recalibrated on the same 12 fields (realised test rate [[e34s.fa_v2_r3|f3]] per picture at the 0.03 setting). Test 1, real asteroids: 16 new fields drawn by seeded asteroid number from JPL Horizons ephemerides as seen from SPHEREx, 8 with V 13–16 and 8 with V 16–17.5. Of [[e34s.ast_n_V17p5]] sightings, v2 marks [[e34s.ast_v2_hits_V17p5]] (95% [[e34s.ast_v2_lo_V17p5|f2]]–[[e34s.ast_v2_hi_V17p5|f2]]), v1 [[e34s.ast_v1_hits_V17p5]], M3b with flags at the median-reference thresholds [[e34s.ast_M3BK_hits_V17p5]], M2 [[e34s.ast_M2K_hits_V17p5]], and the no-flag statistics none. The target, a lower bound above 0.5, was not met. Described without a test rule, using S/N predicted from the catalogue magnitude with the PSF FWHM fixed at 7.5″: of sightings predicted above 10 times the single-picture noise, v2 marks [[e34s.astsnr_v2_hits_gt10]] of [[e34s.astsnr_n_gt10]], where M3b with flags marks all of them, so it still misses some bright asteroids. Test 2, [[e34s.star_n_trials]] real-star transplants on the 20 test fields: recall at S/N 3–5, 5–8, 8–12, 12–20, 20–100 and > 100 is [[e34s.star_recall_v2_s3_5|f2]], [[e34s.star_recall_v2_s5_8|f2]], [[e34s.star_recall_v2_s8_12|f2]], [[e34s.star_recall_v2_s12_20|f2]], [[e34s.star_recall_v2_s20_100|f2]] and [[e34s.star_recall_v2_sgt100|f2]], against [[e34s.star_recall_M3BF_s3_5|f2]], [[e34s.star_recall_M3BF_s5_8|f2]], [[e34s.star_recall_M3BF_s8_12|f2]], [[e34s.star_recall_M3BF_s12_20|f2]], [[e34s.star_recall_M3BF_s20_100|f2]] and [[e34s.star_recall_M3BF_sgt100|f2]] for M3b with flags.

