## 4. Stacking along a known path {#stacking}

An object too faint for any single picture can be measured by combining many pictures at its predicted positions. With amplitude $a_i \pm \sigma_i$ at the predicted position in picture $i$,

$$
\hat{A} = \frac{\sum_i w_i\,a_i}{\sum_i w_i},
\qquad
w_i = \sigma_i^{-2},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}\left(\hat{A}\right) = \left(\sum_i w_i\right)^{-1},
$$

so with equal errors the signal-to-noise grows as $\sqrt{N}$: $\hat{A}/\sigma_{\hat{A}} = \sqrt{N}\,a/\sigma$. Each $a_i$ comes from fitting the target jointly with every Gaia star and the background (Section 1). An earlier version dropped Gaia stars within 0.7 pixel of the prediction as if they were the target, and a neighbour of WISE 0855−0714 leaked into its amplitude in [[e4.passes.2.neighbour_restored.n_frames_with_star_restored]] April–May pictures; every star is now modelled. When the expected brightness varies, as for an asteroid, the code uses the matched filter with template $p_i$, the predicted flux:

$$
\hat{\alpha} = \frac{\sum_i p_i\,a_i/\sigma_i^2}{\sum_i p_i^2/\sigma_i^2},
\qquad
\sigma_{\hat{\alpha}} = \left(\sum_i \frac{p_i^2}{\sigma_i^2}\right)^{-1/2},
$$

which is measured brightness in units of the prediction, and reduces to $\hat{A}$ for $p_i = 1$, the form used for WISE 0855−0714. For asteroids, $p_i$ comes from $H$, $G$ and, where a size and albedo are catalogued, a thermal model; positions come from JPL Horizons with SPHEREx as observer.

**Empty-path null.** The formal errors miss neighbour residuals, PSF undersampling and correlated noise, so $\hat{A}/\sigma_{\hat{A}}$ is not a Gaussian significance. The same pictures are combined along the path displaced by 5–9 pixels at 40 angles each, with the scene refitted at every position. With $\zeta_j$ the null paths' nominal S/N,

$$
z_{\mathrm{null}} = \frac{\hat{A}/\sigma_{\hat{A}} - \operatorname{mean}_j \zeta_j}{\operatorname{std}_j \zeta_j},
\qquad
p = \frac{1 + \#\left\{j : \zeta_j \ge \hat{A}/\sigma_{\hat{A}}\right\}}{N_\mathrm{null} + 1}.
$$

**Why 200 paths.** The smallest attainable $p$ is $1/(N_\mathrm{null}+1)$, and the null spread is uncertain by about $1/\sqrt{2(N_\mathrm{null}-1)}$. Twenty-four paths, our first choice, cannot show $p$ below 1/25 and leave the spread uncertain by about 15%. Two hundred give a floor of $p = $ [[e4bs.pass2_empirical_p_upper|f3]] and about 5%. For WISE 0855−0714 the 200-path spread was [[e4bs.pass2_null200_std|f2]], against [[e4bs.pass2_null24_std|f2]] from 24 paths.

**WISE 0855−0714.** The path uses the position, proper motion and parallax ([[e4.astrometry.parallax_arcsec|f3]]″) of Luhman & Esplin (2016), as seen from the Earth. Pictures at 4.4–5.0 µm are grouped into passes split at gaps over 30 days. In November–December 2025, [[e4bs.pass2_n_pictures]] pictures give a nominal S/N of [[e4bs.pass2_snr_nominal|f1]]. Against 200 empty paths (mean [[e4bs.pass2_null200_mean|f2]], spread [[e4bs.pass2_null200_std|f2]]) that is $z_\mathrm{null} = $ [[e4bs.pass2_sig_200|f1]], with [[e4bs.pass2_null200_n_ge_real]] paths as bright. The earlier [[e4bs.pass2_sig_public_24|f1]]σ against 24 paths does not stand; [[e4bs.pass2_sig_200|f1]]σ is the figure we use. The flux density is [[e4.passes.1.a_hat_mJy|f2]] mJy with a formal error of [[e4.passes.1.sigma_a_mJy|f3]] mJy; the empirical error is larger by the null spread's factor of [[e4bs.pass2_null200_std|f2]]. In April–May 2026 one artefact picture widens the null spread to [[e4bs.pass3_null200_std|f1]], and the detection is [[e4bs.pass3_sig_200|f1]]σ, although no null path is as bright.

![Signal-to-noise as pictures are combined](figs/v2/fig_reveal.svg "a, signal-to-noise ratio of asteroid 98397 against the number of pictures combined along its orbit, with the √N expectation. b, signal-to-noise ratio of single pictures and of empty paths beside the orbit.")

**Faint asteroids.** Of [[e13.n_stacked]] asteroids with a null-calibrated stack, [[e13.n_no_single_5sigma]] have no single picture at 5σ; [[e13.n_combined_ge5]] of these exceed 5σ when combined, and all [[e13.n_ge5_pass_split]] are seen in both the odd and even pictures and peak at the predicted position. Against 200 empty paths, the four stacks above 4σ beat every null ($z_\mathrm{null}$ = [[e13b.results.0.z|f1]], [[e13b.results.1.z|f1]], [[e13b.results.2.z|f1]], [[e13b.results.3.z|f1]]). Those stacks existed before the selection rule, so a blind test followed. [[e14.n_analysed]] main-belt asteroids were drawn at random, and each one's expected S/N, $\sqrt{\sum_i p_i^2/\sigma_i^2}$, was recorded before any stack was computed. Of [[e14.n_eligible]] eligible (no single picture at 5σ, expected S/N ≥ 7), [[e14.n_eligible_ge5]] reached 5σ ([[e14.fraction|pct]], 95% interval [[e14.fraction_ci95.0|pct]]–[[e14.fraction_ci95.1|pct]]), short of the 70% required. The median brightness relative to prediction was [[e14.median_amp_ratio|f2]] ([[e14.median_amp_ratio_ci95.0|f2]]–[[e14.median_amp_ratio_ci95.1|f2]]), inside the required 0.7–1.3, and none of [[e14.false_alarm.n_tracks]] empty paths reached 5σ. The test failed on depth because the stacked noise on empty paths is [[e14.results.8.null_scale|f1]]–[[e14.results.4.null_scale|f1]] times the formal error, so formal errors overstate a stack's depth. Every stack significance on this site uses the empty-path spread.

![Predicted against measured stack signal](figs/v2/e14_pred_vs_meas.svg "Measured signal-to-noise of the combined pictures against the value predicted from the brightness model and the empty-path noise, for the randomly drawn asteroids (filled) and the earlier sample (open). The line marks equality.")

## 5. Astrometry and parallax {#astrometry}

A nearby star traces a small ellipse on the sky over a year as the Earth orbits; measuring that ellipse from SPHEREx positions gives its distance.

**Positions.** In each picture the target is moved over a ±1.5-pixel grid in 0.05-pixel steps, the scene is refitted at each step, and the minimum-$\chi^2$ position is kept. *Simplification:* positions are quantised at 0.05 pixel (0.31″), below the per-picture scatter. Because many pictures in a visit can share a grid value, the clipping scale below is floored at half a step.

**Model.** With tangent-plane offsets $(\Delta\alpha^\ast, \Delta\delta)$ in arcseconds about a fixed point,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta\alpha^\ast(t) &= \Delta\alpha^\ast_0 + \mu_{\alpha^\ast}\,(t - t_0) + \varpi\,P_\alpha(t),\\
\Delta\delta(t) &= \Delta\delta_0 + \mu_{\delta}\,(t - t_0) + \varpi\,P_\delta(t),
\end{aligned}
\qquad
\begin{aligned}
P_\alpha &= X\sin\alpha - Y\cos\alpha,\\
P_\delta &= X\cos\alpha\sin\delta + Y\sin\alpha\sin\delta - Z\cos\delta,
\end{aligned}
$$

where $(X, Y, Z)$ is the Earth's barycentric position in au at mid-exposure (Astropy's built-in ephemeris) and $t_0$ the mean epoch. SPHEREx's low orbit changes a parallax by less than 0.02 mas. The first pass weights by formal errors; pictures more than 5 robust σ (floored at 0.155″) from their visit's median residual are dropped. In the second pass each picture's error is the scatter of the remaining residuals in its visit, and the covariance is multiplied by the reduced $\chi^2$ if above 1. A 1,000-draw bootstrap over pictures gives a second error.

**Comparison-star null.** The decisive check is the same code on the same pictures applied to [[e12.null.summary.n_used]] isolated Gaia stars ($10.5 \le G \le 13$, within 0.3°, drawn by a fixed seed), with $\Delta\varpi = \varpi_\mathrm{fit} - \varpi_\mathrm{Gaia}$. The adopted error is the largest of the fit error, the bootstrap spread and the null stars' root-mean-square $\Delta\varpi$ about zero. All [[e12.null.summary.n_used]] null stars fall below Gaia: mean [[e12.null.summary.mean_mas|f0]] ± [[e12.null.summary.mean_err_mas|f0]] mas, standard deviation [[e12.null.summary.std_mas|f0]], root-mean-square about zero [[e12.null.summary.rms_about_zero_mas|f0]] mas. This is a shared east–west shift of a few hundredths of an arcsecond between spring and autumn visits, of unknown cause, which mimics a negative parallax. It is not subtracted: that would assume the much redder target shares the offset of blue stars, and the adopted error already contains it.

**Epsilon Indi B**, a pair of T dwarfs 0.7–0.8″ apart that SPHEREx sees as one source, has [[e12.fit.n_selected]] pictures with S/N > 20 in four visits, [[e12.fit.n_used]] after clipping. The fit gives $\varpi = $ [[e12.parallax_mas|f0]] ± [[e12.parallax_err_mas.adopted|f0]] mas, [[e12.sigma_from_zero|f1]]σ from zero; the fit error ([[e12.parallax_err_mas.fit_scaled|f0]] mas) and bootstrap spread ([[e12.parallax_err_mas.bootstrap|f0]] mas) are smaller than the null scatter that sets the error. The $\chi^2$ of the visit means is [[e12.fit.chi2_window_means_5par|f1]] with parallax and [[e12.fit.chi2_window_means_no_parallax|f0]] without. The distance is [[e12.distance_pc|f2]] pc ([[e12.distance_ly|f1]] light-years, 68% interval [[e12.distance_interval_ly_68.0|f1]]–[[e12.distance_interval_ly_68.1|f1]]). Gaia DR3 gives [[e12.gaia_parallax_mas|f1]] mas for Ba, slightly biased by its orbit, and [[e12.secondary_reference.parallax_mas|f1]] mas for the system through Epsilon Indi A; the differences are [[e12.sigma_from_gaia|f1]]σ and [[e12.secondary_reference.sigma_from_reference|f1]]σ. The proper motion is ([[e12.proper_motion.pmra_mas_yr|f0]] ± [[e12.proper_motion.pmra_err|f0]], [[e12.proper_motion.pmdec_mas_yr|f0]] ± [[e12.proper_motion.pmdec_err|f0]]) mas yr⁻¹ against Gaia's ([[e12.proper_motion.gaia_pmra|f0]], [[e12.proper_motion.gaia_pmdec|f0]]).

Three limits apply. Picture coordinates come from the pipeline's astrometric solution, which is tied to Gaia reference stars; "from SPHEREx alone" means no Gaia position, motion or parallax of this object is used. The unresolved pair's photocentre moves with its 11-year orbit and depends on wavelength: a steady [[motion_epsindib.passes.2.ddec_arcsec|f1]]″ declination offset from Gaia's position for Ba, the same in every visit, matches the photocentre predicted from the published orbit (Chen et al. 2022). And the first and last visits are a year apart, so the parallax rests mainly on October 2025.

**Red comparison stars.** A colour-dependent position error would give red sources a different offset from the blue null stars, so the same fit is applied to [[e29s.red_set_usable]] Gaia stars with $G_\mathrm{BP} - G_\mathrm{RP} > 2.5$ in the same pictures.

No field around Epsilon Indi B within 0.3° holds a star this red, so the search was extended to 1.3°. The fit of the [[e29s.primary_blue_n]] blue stars reproduces the earlier values exactly. Relative to Gaia, the red stars' parallaxes are offset by [[e29s.primary_red_mean|f1]] ± [[e29s.primary_red_se|f1]] mas and the blue stars' by [[e29s.primary_blue_mean|f1]] ± [[e29s.primary_blue_se|f1]] mas. The difference, [[e29s.primary_D_red_minus_blue_mas|f1]] ± [[e29s.primary_SE_D_mas|f1]] mas ([[e29s.primary_D_over_SE|f2]] standard errors; 95% interval [[e29s.primary_ci95_D_mas_0|f1]] to [[e29s.primary_ci95_D_mas_1|f1]] mas), is below the threshold of two standard errors, so no colour correction is applied. Across all stars the offset changes by [[e29s.descriptive_line_vs_bprp_slope_mas_per_mag|f1]] ± [[e29s.descriptive_line_vs_bprp_slope_err|f1]] mas per magnitude of colour. The reddest comparison star has $G_\mathrm{BP} - G_\mathrm{RP} = 3.1$ and Epsilon Indi B has [[e29s.target_bp_rp|f1]]. The test therefore excludes a gradual colour trend up to that colour, but not an effect peculiar to a T dwarf's spectrum. A second, wavelength test fits detectors 1–3 and 4–6 separately. The comparison stars' parallaxes from detectors 1–3 are higher by [[e29s.secondary_comparison_pooled_mean|f0]] ± [[e29s.secondary_comparison_pooled_se|f0]] mas, equally for red and blue stars, so most of the shared offset sits in the long-wavelength detectors. Epsilon Indi B's two halves ([[e29s.secondary_target_halves_D13_parallax_mas|f0]] ± [[e29s.secondary_target_halves_D13_err_mas|f0]] and [[e29s.secondary_target_halves_D46_parallax_mas|f0]] ± [[e29s.secondary_target_halves_D46_err_mas|f0]] mas) differ in the same way as the comparison stars' ($z = $ [[e29s.secondary_target_z|f2]]).

![Parallax offset against colour](figs/v2/e29_colour.svg "Parallax from SPHEREx positions minus the Gaia parallax, against Gaia BP − RP colour, for the [[e29s.primary_blue_n]] blue and [[e29s.red_set_usable]] red comparison stars and for Epsilon Indi B. Epsilon Indi B's bar is its fit error only, without the comparison-star scatter; other bars are 1σ.")

![Parallax of Epsilon Indi B](figs/v2/e12_parallax.svg "a, positions over a year: single pictures (small points), visit averages (circles), and fits with proper motion only (dotted) and with parallax (dash-dot). b, visit averages with the proper motion removed, and the fitted parallax ellipse. Bars are the standard error of each visit mean.")

## 6. Spectra {#spectra}

A SPHEREx spectrum is assembled from many pictures, each giving one flux at the wavelength where the source fell.

**Channel averaging.** Fluxes $f_i \pm \sigma_i$ are binned by the central wavelength at the source and combined as $F_b = \sum_i f_i\sigma_i^{-2} / \sum_i \sigma_i^{-2}$. The error comes from the scatter. Per object, $s = \max\left(1, 1.4826\,\operatorname{MAD}\{(f_i - F_b)/\sigma_i\}\right)$, pictures beyond $5s$ are dropped once, and the bin error is $s\left(\sum_i\sigma_i^{-2}\right)^{-1/2}$. For WISE 0855−0714 the error is the larger of the formal error and the bin's spread along 24 empty paths. The browser tool's quick-look spectrum, a 3 × 3-pixel sum on the resampled grid without aperture correction, recovers [[e31s.ql_fov2_D6_ours_ratio|pct]]–[[e31s.ql_fov2_D1_ours_ratio|pct]] of the fitted flux in its default 2′ view, depending on detector; the inverse ratios are its per-detector corrections.

**Comparison with JWST.** The SPHEREx spectrum of WISE 0855−0714 in 0.1 µm steps from 3.8 to 5.0 µm is divided by the JWST NIRSpec PRISM spectrum of Luhman et al. (2024) averaged over the same steps. The inverse-variance mean ratio over the [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.n_bins_detected]] steps detected at 3σ is [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.mean_ratio_spherex_over_jwst|f2]] ± [[e11.targets.wise0855.jwst.comparison.PRISM.mean_ratio_err|f2]]. The two reddest steps, 4.8–5.0 µm, fall below JWST for reasons not established. The JWST level was tied to Spitzer by its authors, so agreement in level is not fully independent; agreement in shape is. A SPHEREx spectrum of this object is already published (Rustamkulov et al. 2026); ours is an independent check. *Simplification:* a pixel does not see a 0.1 µm box but a narrow band of equivalent width $\Delta\lambda_i$ around $\lambda_{c,i}$. The comparison was therefore repeated with JWST passed through each picture's response $T_i$, approximated as a Gaussian of that centre and equivalent width because the laboratory response curves were not available, and combined with the SPHEREx weights:

$$
P_i = \frac{\int F_\nu^{\mathrm{JWST}}(\lambda)\,T_i(\lambda)\,\lambda^{-1}\,d\lambda}{\int T_i(\lambda)\,\lambda^{-1}\,d\lambda},
\qquad
P_b = \frac{\sum_i P_i\,\sigma_i^{-2}}{\sum_i \sigma_i^{-2}}.
$$

The ratio becomes [[e32s.r_new|f3]] ± [[e32s.r_new_err|f3]], against [[e32s.r_old|f3]] ± [[e32s.r_old_err|f3]] for the box average. Plausible changes of the response shape and width, and a ±10 nm shift of the band centre, move it by [[e32s.shift_min_plausible|f3]] to +[[e32s.shift_max_plausible|f3]], well inside the statistical error; the band centre matters more than the shape. The two reddest steps stay low.

![Spectrum of WISE 0855−0714](figs/v2/e11_wise0855.svg "SPHEREx spectrum in 0.1 µm steps (circles; open circles are below 3σ; bars are 1σ), the JWST NIRSpec spectrum (thin line) averaged over the same steps (squares), and the Spitzer 4.5 µm measurement (diamond).")

**Methane index.** Methane absorbs at 3.3 µm in T and Y dwarfs but not in early L dwarfs. From bins at 2.9–3.1, 3.2–3.4 and 3.9–4.1 µm,

$$
I_{\mathrm{CH_4}} = \frac{F_{33}}{\tfrac12\left(F_{30} + F_{40}\right)},
\qquad
\left(\frac{\sigma_I}{I}\right)^2 = \left(\frac{\sigma_{33}}{F_{33}}\right)^2 + \frac{\sigma_{30}^2 + \sigma_{40}^2}{\left(F_{30} + F_{40}\right)^2},
$$

undefined if the denominator is below twice its error. The calibration set is [[e15.calibration.n]] dwarfs with spectroscopic types from the 20-pc census (Kirkpatrick et al. 2021), five in each of L0–L4.5, L5–L9.5, T0–T4.5, T5–T9.5 and Y, drawn by a fixed seed. The threshold is the midpoint of the interval of cuts misclassifying the fewest calibration objects under "$I < \theta$ means T or later": $\theta = $ [[e15.calibration.threshold|f3]]. An object is methane-bearing if $(\theta - I)/\sigma_I \ge 3$, L-like if $(I - \theta)/\sigma_I \ge 3$, otherwise undetermined. Leaving each object out and recomputing $\theta$, [[e15.calibration.n_decisive]] of [[e15.calibration.n]] receive a decisive call and [[e15.calibration.loo_accuracy|pct]] of those are correct; both errors are at the L/T transition. Late-L dwarfs already show some 3.3 µm absorption, so the boundary sits near L9–T1. For faint objects, fits at 12 empty positions show the scatter-based errors are too small, so calls on faint objects use the empty-position error.

![Methane index](figs/v2/e15_methane.svg "The 4.4–5.0 µm to 1.0–1.3 µm flux ratio against the methane index, for brown dwarfs of known spectral type and for candidates without a published spectrum. The dashed line is the dividing value; bars are 1σ.")

**Hydrogen-line excess.** SPHEREx covers Paschen-α (1.875 µm, detector 3) and Brackett-α (4.052 µm, detector 5). In each visit, line pictures are those whose channel contains the line, $\lvert\lambda_i - \lambda_L\rvert \le \tfrac12\Delta\lambda_i$. Continuum pictures lie more than one bandwidth away, within 20% of the line wavelength and clear of other hydrogen and helium lines, with at least three on each side. A straight continuum $C(\lambda)$ is fitted to them by weighted least squares with one 5σ clip, and for each line picture

$$
r_i = \frac{f_i}{C(\lambda_i)} - 1,
\qquad
\sigma_{r,i} = \frac{\sqrt{\sigma_i^2 + \left(f_i/C\right)^2\sigma_C^2}}{C}.
$$

The visit excess $E_w$ is the inverse-variance mean of $r_i$ ($z_w = E_w/\sigma_{E,w}$), and the star's excess $E$ the mean over visits ($z = E/\sigma_E$). Calibration uses control stars, ordinary Gaia stars of similar brightness and colour 20″–200″ away and so in the same pictures:

$$
Z = \frac{z - \operatorname{median}_{\mathrm{ctrl}}(z)}{1.4826\,\operatorname{MAD}_{\mathrm{ctrl}}(z)},
$$

and likewise $Z_w$. A line is detected if $Z \ge 5$ and two or more visits, each with its own continuum, have $Z_w \ge 2$. Median and MAD ignore the tail, so the false-positive rate measured on the same controls is a fair test. The sample was [[e20.n_candidates]] stars that Gaia DR3 classifies as Herbig Ae/Be or Be stars, drawn by a fixed seed, with [[e20.n_controls]] controls; the rule was meant to pass at most 2% of controls. [[e20.control_fp]] of [[e20.control_testable]] controls pass ([[e20.control_fp_rate|pct]], 95% upper limit [[e20.control_fp_rate_upper95|pct]]), and [[e20.N_total]] of [[e20.M_total]] testable candidates show an excess, with [[e20.expected_fp_total|f1]] expected by chance. The median excess is [[e20.median_frac_excess_detected_Paa|pct]] of the continuum at Paschen-α and [[e20.median_frac_excess_detected_Bra|pct]] at Brackett-α. At a line-free wavelength (4.25 µm) the rule passes no candidate. At a resolving power near 40, stellar emission cannot be separated from emission by surrounding gas.

![Hydrogen-line excess](figs/v2/e20_lines.svg "a, b, flux relative to the local continuum around Paschen-α (a) and Brackett-α (b) for one candidate (stars) and a control star in the same pictures (crosses); bars are 1σ. c, distribution of the line excess for candidates and controls; the dashed line is the detection threshold.")

**Censuses.** Both rules, unchanged, were applied to candidates without published classifications: the methane rule to ultracool-dwarf candidates and the line rule to new Gaia emission-line candidates.

The methane rule, unchanged (threshold 0.356; a call needs the index at least 3σ from it, with errors from 12 empty positions around each object), was applied to [[e22s.n_measured]] brown-dwarf candidates from published lists: [[e22s.n_sample_tier1]] from Kirkpatrick et al. (2021, 2024) and Meisner et al. (2020a, b), and [[e22s.n_sample_tier2]] photometric L/T candidates from Dal Ponte et al. (2023). [[e22s.n_decisive]] were bright enough for a decisive call: [[e22s.n_T_or_later]] "T or later" and [[e22s.n_L_like]] "L-like". As a control, [[e22s.control_n]] dwarfs with spectroscopic types L0–L8.5 were measured the same way, and [[e22s.control_n_false_T_or_later]] were called "T or later". Among the [[e22s.tier2_photL_n_decisive]] decisive objects photometrically typed L, [[e22s.tier2_photL_n_T_or_later]] were called "T or later". For [[e22s.spiff_group_n_decisive_with_type]] objects already typed from SPHEREx spectra by another team, our call agreed [[e22s.spiff_group_n_agree]] times. We searched SIMBAD, the VizieR tables of Kirkpatrick et al. (2024) and Meisner et al. (2020b), the SPIFF and SUDA tables, and arXiv ([[e22s.search_date]]), and found no published spectral type for any of the [[e22s.n_candidates_no_published_type]] "T or later" objects. One, WISE 0628−8057, already has a type from methane imaging (Tinney et al. 2018). Most candidates lie 3–4.7σ below the threshold, and the strongest is [[e22s.cand1_name]] ([[e22s.cand1_sigma|f1]]σ). The number of false calls expected under the original rule is not well bounded: the 95% upper limit is [[e22s.expected_false_95_upper|f0]], because it allows every decisive object to be an L dwarf. A bound computed afterwards, pooling the photometric-L objects with the known L dwarfs, is [[e22s.posthoc_expected_false_95_upper|f1]]. Each candidate needs a spectrum to confirm its type.

The line rule, unchanged, was applied to stars that Gaia DR3 classifies as Be or T Tauri candidates (none of them drawn in the first test), each with matched comparison stars in the same pictures. Measurement stopped at the submission deadline after the first [[e23s.e23_fields_measured]] of [[e23s.e23_fields_selected]] selected fields in a fixed random order, so the cut does not depend on any measured value. [[e23s.e23_control_fp]] of [[e23s.e23_control_testable]] comparison stars pass ([[e23s.e23_control_fp_rate|pct]]; 95% upper limit [[e23s.e23_control_fp_rate_upper95|pct]]), below the 2% the rule allows. Line emission is detected in [[e23s.e23_N_beStar]] of [[e23s.e23_M_beStar]] testable Be candidates and [[e23s.e23_N_TTauri]] of [[e23s.e23_M_TTauri]] T Tauri candidates, [[e23s.e23_N_total]] of [[e23s.e23_M_total]] in total, with [[e23s.e23_expected_fp|f1]] false detections expected. Of the detected stars, [[e23s.e23_n_no_simbad_emission_type]] have no emission-line or young-star type in SIMBAD; most of these are already in published Hα surveys. We searched SIMBAD, VSX and [[deriveds.e23_n_vizier_catalogues|int]] VizieR catalogues of emission-line, Be, Herbig, T Tauri, young and Hα-emitting stars, and found no published emission-line classification for [[e23s.e23_n_no_published_emission_classification_found]] stars, all Be candidates. Six of them are detected in Brackett-α only, most close to the threshold. They are candidates.

## 7. Variability {#variability}

To measure brightness changes, each source's spectrum, sampled at a different wavelength in every picture, must first be removed; what remains is a light curve.

**Static-spectrum model.** Per source and detector, the flux is a cubic B-spline in wavelength with four equally spaced interior knots,

$$
M_i = \sum_{j=1}^{8} \gamma_{d(i),j}\,B_j\left(\lambda_i\right),
$$

fitted by weighted least squares with the Section 1 errors; three iterations exclude pictures beyond 5σ from the fit only. The residual is $a_i = (f_i - M_i)/M_i$ with weight $w_i = (M_i/\sigma_i)^2$; in one-day bins with at least three pictures, $a_b = \sum w_i a_i / \sum w_i$ and $\sigma_b = \left(\sum w_i\right)^{-1/2}$.

**Statistics.** Four per source:

$$
\chi^2_r = \frac{1}{n - n_\mathrm{par}}\sum_i \left(\frac{f_i - M_i}{\sigma_i}\right)^2,
\qquad
\chi^2_{r,\mathrm{det}} = \max_d \chi^2_{r,d},
\qquad
s_2 = \frac{1}{N_b}\sum_b \left(\frac{a_b}{\sigma_b}\right)^2,
$$

and the maximum Lomb–Scargle power of $a_i$ over 0.01–10 d⁻¹, excluding periods near 1, 1/2 and 1/3 d, half a year, a year, multiples of the 98-minute orbit and the source's own window peaks. Each is normalised for brightness: with $y = \log_{10}X$, its median and $1.4826\,\operatorname{MAD}$ on calibration stars in ten magnitude bins give $z = (y - \operatorname{med})/\operatorname{scale}$. The threshold is the larger of the 99.9th percentiles of $z$ over the calibration stars and over the same stars with time labels shuffled within each detector. A source is above threshold if any statistic is, a nominal rate of at most 0.4% per source.

**Calibration on non-variable stars.** Within 0.4° of the north ecliptic pole, observed on almost every orbit, [[e18bs.n_pictures|int]] pictures measure [[e18a.data.n_reported|int]] catalogued sources. Gaia stars not listed as variable by Gaia DR3, VSX or Kim et al. (2026), and not in the Milliquas quasar catalogue, form the null population. It is split at random: the calibration half sets thresholds and errors, and the test half measures the false-alarm rate, [[e18bs.fa_k]] of [[e18bs.fa_n]] ([[e18bs.fa_rate|pct]], 95% upper limit [[e18bs.fa_hi|pct]]). Recovery is measured by injecting sinusoids $f\,[1 + A\sin(2\pi t/P + \phi)]$ and 8%-duty box eclipses of depth $A$ into test-half stars before the static fit, with $A$ = 1–20% and $P$ log-uniform in 0.2–50 d. The floor in each 1-mag bin is the smallest $A$ recovered at least half the time.

**Learned correction for shared systematics.** All stars in a picture share errors: zero points differ between pictures, the response varies across each detector, and slow drifts run through the year. The fractional correction for picture $i$ of source $s$ on detector $d$ is

$$
\hat{a}_i = \theta_{p(i),0} + \theta_{p(i),1}\,\ell_i + m_d\left(x_i, y_i\right) + \sum_{k=1}^{K} a_{s k}\,c_{k}\left(t_{j(i)}\right).
$$

Here $\theta_{p,0}$ is a zero point per picture $p$, and $\theta_{p,1}$ multiplies the clipped log brightness $\ell_i$, so the zero point may depend on brightness. $m_d$ is a map in 16 × 16 cells per detector, and $c_k(t_j)$ are $K$ cotrending vectors on a daily grid with per-source coefficients $a_{sk}$. Picture terms and maps are fitted jointly by weighted least squares, with backfitting and one re-clip at 5σ. The vectors come from SysRem (Tamuz, Mazeh & Zucker 2005), applied to the day-binned residuals $r_{sj}$ left after the picture terms:

$$
\min_{\{a_{sk}\},\{c_{kj}\}} \;\sum_{s,j} \frac{\left(r_{sj} - \sum_{k=1}^{K} a_{sk}\,c_{kj}\right)^2}{\sigma_{sj}^2}.
$$

*Simplification:* as in SysRem, components are found one at a time by alternating weighted least squares (30 iterations) and then subtracted, which approximates the joint minimum. When the correction is applied, each source's $K$ coefficients are refitted jointly to its own residuals, and $f_i' = f_i/(1 + \hat{a}_i)$.

**Selection by held-out χ².** Only the [[e18bs.n_null]] calibration-half null stars train the model, split by star into two folds; every choice is scored on the fold not used to learn it. Among eight picture models, held-out per-picture $\chi^2$ chose the zero point with brightness term plus the detector map. $K$ is the smallest value within 1% of the minimum held-out day-binned $\chi^2$. For that score, vectors are learned on one fold, and each held-out star's coefficients are fitted on even days and scored on odd days, then the reverse. The result is $K = $ [[e26s.n_components]]. A calibration star is corrected by the model from the other fold. Injections go only into test-half stars, in the order inject, correct, compute statistics. The only quantities fitted to an injected light curve are its own $K$ coefficients, so any signal they absorb is counted in the measured recovery.

**Real variable stars.** Tested on real stars across the sky, SPHEREx alone recovers [[e10.recall.testable_variables.k]] of [[e10.recall.testable_variables.n]] catalogued variable stars that it can test (long-period variables: [[e10.recall.by_type.LPV.testable.k]] of [[e10.recall.by_type.LPV.testable.n]]), while flagging [[e10.false_positive.test_half_T.k]] of [[e10.false_positive.test_half_T.n]] steady comparison stars. R Draconis (below) peaked [[e28s.rdra_D6_offset_from_prediction_d|f1]] days from the date predicted from earlier pictures. The per-star test is described in Section 10. Variations planted into real light curves, below, only set the depth.

**Floors before and after.** Test-half false alarms are [[e26s.fa_before]] before and [[e26s.fa_after]] after ([[e26s.fa_after_rate|pct]]). Sinusoid floors:

| AB magnitude | 15–16 | 16–17 | 17–18 | 18–19 | 19–20 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Before | [[e26s.floor_sin_before_15_16|pct]] | [[e26s.floor_sin_before_16_17|pct]] | [[e26s.floor_sin_before_17_18|pct]] | [[e26s.floor_sin_before_18_19|pct]] | above 20% |
| After | [[e26s.floor_sin_after_15_16|pct]] | [[e26s.floor_sin_after_16_17|pct]] | [[e26s.floor_sin_after_17_18|pct]] | [[e26s.floor_sin_after_18_19|pct]] | [[e26s.floor_sin_after_19_20|pct]] |

At 12–15 mag the floor is [[e26s.floor_sin_after_12_13|pct]] before and after, the smallest amplitude tested. Of identical injections, [[e26.post_hoc.paired_injections.all.gained|int]] were recovered only after correction and [[e26.post_hoc.paired_injections.all.lost]] only before. No floor rose; eclipse floors did not change. Catalogued variables above threshold went from [[e26s.recall_catalogued_before]] to [[e26s.recall_catalogued_after]]; most are rotating or solar-like stars whose infrared amplitudes, near 0.1%, are below the floor. A shared signal at 79 and 95 days is reduced ([[e26s.common_mode_before]] → [[e26s.common_mode_after]] sources peaking there) but not removed, so such periods are not treated as candidates.

![Depth limit for variability](figs/v2/e26_floor.svg "Depth limit: fraction of planted sinusoidal variations recovered against source brightness, without (a) and with (b) the correction for shared errors, for amplitudes of 1% to 20%. Shaded bands are 95% intervals; the dotted line marks half recovered.")

**A periodic test: R Draconis.** This Mira variable (catalogued period [[e28s.rdra_period_d|f0]] d) saturates the detector. It is therefore measured from unsaturated pixels 7–12 pixels from its centre, with errors from 43 comparison stars measured the same way. A sine fit to an earlier set of pictures predicted a 4.4–5.0 µm maximum on [[e28s.rdra_predicted_max_mjd|date]]; the test uses only pictures outside that set. Per detector, 30-day bins are fitted as

$$
\ln\left(1 + a_{d,b}\right) = c_0 + c_1\cos\frac{2\pi t}{P} + c_2\sin\frac{2\pi t}{P},
\qquad
A = \sqrt{c_1^2 + c_2^2},
\qquad
z_A = \frac{A}{k\,\sigma_A},
$$

with $k = $ [[e28s.rdra_k_6|f2]] measured on the comparison stars' light curves, none of which reaches $z_A = 5$. It passes if $z_A \ge 5$ and the maximum falls within 15 days of the prediction. At 4.4–5.0 µm, [[e28.rdra.n_pictures_by_detector.6|int]] new pictures give a semi-amplitude of [[e28s.rdra_D6_semi_amplitude_mag|f2]] ± [[e28s.rdra_D6_semi_amplitude_mag_err|f2]] mag ($z_A = $ [[e28s.rdra_D6_z_amplitude|f1]]) and a maximum on [[e28s.rdra_D6_t_max_mjd|date]] ± [[e28s.rdra_D6_t_max_err_d|f0]] d, [[e28s.rdra_D6_offset_from_prediction_d|f1]] d after the prediction. The sine is incomplete ($\chi^2$ = [[e28s.rdra_D6_chi2_sine|f0]] for [[e28s.rdra_D6_dof_sine]] degrees of freedom); with errors scaled to the scatter about the fit, the amplitude is [[e28s.rdra_posthoc_D6_z_errors_scaled_by_fit|f1]]σ. R Draconis is more saturated than any comparison star, and the new pictures span the same dates as the old, so a date-dependent instrumental effect would repeat. At 3.8–4.4 µm too few dates are covered for a fit.

![Light curve of R Draconis](figs/v2/e28_rdra.svg "Brightness relative to the mean at 4.4–5.0 µm (a) and 3.8–4.4 µm (b), in 30-day bins: pictures not used for the prediction (filled circles) and pictures used for it (open circles), with the predicted curve (dash-dot), the fitted sine (solid; a only) and the scatter of comparison stars (grey band). Bars are 1σ.")

## 8. Unsupervised anomaly search {#anomaly}

The anomaly search asks, without labels and at a measured false-alarm rate, whether any source near the north ecliptic pole has a spectrum unlike the rest.

**Features.** Pictures are averaged into 17 log-spaced channels per detector (102 in all), each a clipped inverse-variance mean with a 3% per-picture floor and errors inflated by $\sqrt{\chi^2_r}$. The spectrum is normalised by its median ($\mathbf{X}$, errors $\mathbf{S}$). Tree models use $\mathbf{Z}$: 18 coarse continuum values plus the high-pass residual (spectrum minus a seven-channel running median, over its error, clipped at ±30), so narrow features are not hidden by the continuum. The population is [[e18a.data.n_population|int]] sources with S/N ≥ 5. [[e18a.data.n_held_out]] sources of known rare classes (quasars, planetary nebulae, white dwarfs, emission-line and young stars, known variables) are held out of training and calibration and scored like test sources.

**Scorers.** (i) An unsupervised random forest (Baron & Poznanski 2017) of 500 trees separates real feature vectors from copies with each column shuffled independently, so only correlations distinguish them. With $n_t(\mathbf{z})$ the real training sources sharing $\mathbf{z}$'s leaf in tree $t$ (counted only if the leaf is classified real),

$$
s_\mathrm{RF}(\mathbf{z}) = 1 - \frac{1}{T\,n}\sum_{t=1}^{T} n_t(\mathbf{z}),
$$

one minus the mean forest similarity to the training set, Baron & Poznanski's weirdness. (ii) An autoencoder $p \to 64 \to 32 \to 6 \to 32 \to 64 \to p$ (ELU, linear bottleneck, $p$ channels), trained with masked mean-squared error, scores the mean of the five largest channel $\chi^2$ values $(X_c - \hat{X}_c)^2/(S_c^2 + (0.01\hat{X}_c)^2)$, so one- or two-channel features are not diluted. (iii) An isolation forest (Liu, Ting & Zhou 2008) of 500 trees scores $2^{-\mathbb{E}[h(\mathbf{z})]/c(n)}$, with $h$ the isolating path length and $c(n)$ its expectation.

**Conformal $p$-values.** Sources are split into two folds; models trained on one score the other, so scores within a fold are exchangeable. Within each fold and S/N tercile (Mondrian bins), each score becomes

$$
p = \frac{1 + \#\left\{j : s_j^{\mathrm{cal}} \ge s\right\}}{n + 1},
$$

with a population source left out of its own reference set. For a source exchangeable with the reference set its rank is uniform, so $\Pr(p \le \alpha) \le \alpha$ (Vovk, Gammerman & Shafer 2005; Bates et al. 2023). Binning by S/N makes this hold within each bin, so noisy faint spectra cannot crowd out bright ones. The combined score $S = 1 - \tfrac12\left(p_{(1)} + p_{(2)}\right)$, from the two smallest per-model $p$, is a symmetric function of the population, so its conformal rank $p_\mathrm{comb}$ is also valid. A flag requires $p_\mathrm{comb} \le \alpha = $ [[e18a.threshold.alpha|f3]] and at least two models with $p \le $ [[e18a.threshold.alpha_per_model|f2]]; the second condition only removes flags. The control is marginal: about $\alpha$ times the number of null sources are expected to be flagged. Genuine anomalies in the reference set raise everyone else's $p$, which makes the count conservative.

**Injections.** Synthetic rare spectra were added to individual pictures of real hosts before averaging, keeping their sampling, masks and noise. They comprise unresolved hydrogen lines, water and CO₂ ice bands, a real SPHEREx T4.5-dwarf spectrum, a reddened quasar and a carbon star, each at three strengths, plus a null class. The null class is flagged at [[e18a.calibration_checks.null_injection_flag_rate|pct]] (of [[e18a.calibration_checks.null_injection_n]]) and catalogue-clean stars at [[e18a.calibration_checks.clean_star_flag_rate|pct]], against $\alpha$ = 0.5%. The medium T-dwarf spectrum is recovered [[e18as.tdwarf_medium_100_300|pct]] of the time at S/N 100–300. Narrow lines and ices are rarely flagged except in the brightest sources. Flags are triaged only above S/N [[e18a.threshold.reporting_floor_snr|f0]], where the class-averaged medium-strength recovery reaches 25%.

**Result.** There are [[e18a.threshold.n_flags_all_snr]] flags, [[e18a.n_flags]] above the floor, where [[e18a.threshold.expected_false_flags|f1]] are expected by chance. Each was checked for artefacts and against SIMBAD, VSX, Gaia DR3, Milliquas, NEOWISE variables and DESI DR1 redshifts. [[e18as.n_artefact]] are artefacts, three in the halo of the planetary nebula NGC 6543. [[e18as.n_explained]] are star-forming galaxies whose 3.3 µm hydrocarbon band is redshifted into an unusual channel. [[e18as.n_survivor]] remain unexplained, and none of the [[e18as.n_held_out]] held-out known rare sources was flagged. The search is thus sensitive only to strong, broad departures in bright sources. Its flag count is consistent with chance, and no bright source in the field has a spectrum the catalogues we checked fail to explain.

![Injection recovery of the anomaly search](figs/v2/e18a_injections.svg "Fraction of injected rare spectra flagged against host signal-to-noise, by class and strength. The dashed line marks the reporting floor.")

## 9. Moving objects {#movers}

Shift-and-stack looks for asteroids that no catalogue predicts, moving pixels between visits. On real objects it recovers every catalogued asteroid brighter than $V = 17$ in its six fields, and the stacking it builds on is shown on real faint asteroids and on WISE 0855−0714 (Section 4). Planted movers are used only to measure how faint it reaches.

**Shift-and-stack.** Six 0.4° × 0.4° fields within 3° of the ecliptic, away from the Galactic plane and earlier fields, were drawn by a fixed seed. Pictures within ±3 days of a reference time are resampled onto a 6.2″ grid. The static sky is removed with the per-pixel median of the same detector's pictures in other groups (exposures within 0.05 d form a group), so a slow mover cannot subtract itself. Each picture is then matched-filtered with a 1.55-pixel-FWHM Gaussian:

$$
F = \frac{\sum_p g_p w_p r_p}{\sum_p g_p^2 w_p},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(F) = \left(\sum_p g_p^2 w_p\right)^{-1},
$$

in V-equivalent flux $F_V = F\,S_\odot(0.55\,\mu\mathrm{m})/S_\odot(\lambda)$, so a solar-coloured asteroid has the same $F_V$ at every wavelength. Thermal emission is ignored, which is conservative. Variances are rescaled by each picture's robust S/N scatter and by a per-pixel temporal scale. Each exposure's S/N is clipped at ±5, so none can dominate. Along $\mathbf{x}(t) = \mathbf{x}_0 + \mathbf{v}(t - t_\mathrm{ref})$,

$$
\left(\frac{S}{N}\right) = \frac{\sum_e w_e F_e\left(\mathbf{x}(t_e)\right)}{\sqrt{\sum_e w_e}} ,
$$

over [[e17.grid.n_rates|int]] rates of 0.05–1.0° per day in all directions, spaced so the trail error stays below 0.7 pixel. The detection statistic drops the visit contributing most, because in null searches the tail came from single visits holding a source static within that visit. The threshold comes from the same search with exposure times randomly permuted, ten times per field, at 0.1 false candidates per field: $T = $ [[e17.threshold.snr|f2]], giving [[e17.expected_false_candidates|f1]] false candidates expected over six fields. Of [[e17.known.n_searchable]] catalogued asteroids in the patches, the search found the [[e17.known.n_recovered]] brighter than $V = 17$, which are all of them, and none of the fainter ones. The depth limit comes from [[e17s.n_planted|int]] planted movers, of which [[e17s.n_found]] were recovered; a logistic fit reaches half its plateau of [[e17s.cmax|pct]] at $V = $ [[e17s.V_half_of_max|f2]] ± [[e17.injections.fit_all.V50_err|f2]]. Clipping biases measured brightness low, to [[e17.injections.flux_scale_measured_over_true|f2]] of the true value. No uncatalogued candidate survives the filters for split halves, multiple detectors, bright stars, flagged pixels, crosstalk and single-exposure dominance. Permuting single exposures under-represents artefacts coherent within a visit. The flagged-pixel filter was also corrected after the first results were seen, and one catalogued asteroid still fails it.

![Depth limit of shift-and-stack](figs/v2/e17_recovery.svg "Depth limit: fraction of planted moving objects recovered by the shift-and-stack search against V magnitude, with the logistic fit. Bars are 68% intervals.")

## 10. Tested and not adopted {#not-adopted}

Each method below was tested like those above, on data it was not tuned on, and failed its criterion or passed in a form too weak to use.

**Wavelength-aware change model.** At a common threshold it recovers [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M4.efficiency.4|pct]] of planted S/N-20 sources against [[e2_d1.recall_at_k5.M3.efficiency.4|pct]] for the plain median. It flags more changes than one detector at a time in [[e2.n_fields_corr_gt_naive_det]] of 20 random fields (median ratio [[e2.summary.ratio_corr_over_naive_det.median|f2]]). It stays in the tool as an experimental view (Section 2).

**Native-pixel scene reference.** Modelling each picture as Gaia stars plus a plane in native pixels reached a low floor on the calibration field ($f$ = [[e6.f6|f3]]). At its threshold there ($k$ = [[e6.k6|f1]]) it recovered almost no planted sources, and on test fields it gave [[e6.M6_test_false_alarm.rate_per_frame|f3]] false changes per picture against a target of 0.01. Infrared sources missing from Gaia appear as changes in every picture.

**Change maps for moving objects.** Of [[e3.all.n_pairs]] sightings of catalogued asteroids brighter than $V = 19$, the wavelength-aware map found [[e3.all.M4.hits]] and one detector at a time [[e3.all.M2.hits]]. Most were too faint for one picture; masking the OUTLIER flag, which the pipeline sets on movers, removed the brighter ones. With OUTLIER unmasked and thresholds re-set, a blind test on 10 new ecliptic fields found [[e3b.primary.M2.hits]] of [[e3b.primary.n_pairs]] sightings with every method. Faint asteroids are stacked instead (Section 4).

**Astrometric calibration model.** A per-detector position offset was fitted to [[e16.calibration_sample.n_eligible]] Gaia stars in [[e16.calibration_sample.n_fields]] fields unrelated to the parallax, with the model chosen by cross-validation on one half. Tested on the other half ([[e16s.n_stars_B]] stars, [[e16s.n_windows_B]] star-visits), it lowered the root-mean-square visit offset from Gaia from [[e16s.rms_before_mas|f0]] to [[e16s.rms_after_mas|f0]] mas. That is a [[e16s.reduction|pct]] reduction, against the 20% required. Each star's own scatter is several times the correction, so this metric could not fall by 20%. The offset, a few thousandths of a pixel along detector $x$, is confirmed but not applied.

**Barnard's Star.** Its position was measured from the centre of symmetry of its unsaturated wings, with the estimator chosen on 26 comparison stars. The measured northward motion is [[e8.fit.north.pm_arcsec_yr|f2]] ± [[e8.fit.north.pm_err|f2]]″ yr⁻¹, against Gaia's [[e8.gaia.pmdec_mas_yr|k2]]″ yr⁻¹. Faint neighbours in the same pictures, however, share an east–west drift beyond their errors, so the test, which required them to agree with Gaia, failed.

**Per-star change test for saturated stars.** A static spectrum with one free brightness factor per visit reduced comparison-star pulls to unity. With [[e7.null.n_control_stars]] null stars, though, the smallest attainable $p$ was [[e7.null.smallest_attainable_p|f3]], so no verdict was possible. For RW Aurigae the static model fails because of saturated wings ($\chi^2$ of [[e7.targets.rwaur.chi2_static|k0]] thousand for [[e7.targets.rwaur.dof_static]] degrees of freedom); R Draconis left [[e7.targets.rdra.n_usable]] usable pictures. On catalogued variables the test passed its false-alarm part ([[e10.false_positive.test_half_T.k]] of [[e10.false_positive.test_half_T.n]] comparison stars, [[e10.false_positive.test_half_T.rate|pct]]). It recovered [[e10.recall.by_type.LPV.testable.k]] of [[e10.recall.by_type.LPV.testable.n]] long-period variables, but the interval's lower bound did not exceed 50%: inconclusive. Only [[e10.recall.by_type.LPV.testable.n]] of [[e10.recall.by_type.LPV.all.n]] were unsaturated enough to test.

**Bright-star artefact check.** A ring statistic set on [[e19s.n_comparison]] comparison-star pictures (threshold [[e19s.threshold_T|f1]]) did not detect the artefact "shell" reported around VY Canis Majoris at its detector position. Comparing spike patterns after the fact, different bright stars at that position share the pattern (correlation [[e19s.vycma.c_spot|f2]], [[e19s.epsoct.c_spot|f2]], [[e19s.gyuma.c_spot|f2]]), while the same stars elsewhere have medians of [[e19s.vycma.c_else_median|f2]]–[[e19s.gyuma.c_else_median|f2]]. The pattern follows the detector, not the sky. This replicates Karambelkar, Sharma & De (2026).

**AGN variability spectrum and dust lag.** For [[e25.n_agn]] known AGN against [[e25.n_control]] matched controls, no wavelength bin showed an excess variance at 3σ, and one bright Seyfert dominated the weighted mean. A stacked cross-correlation of short- and long-wavelength light curves met its formal criterion at a lag of [[e25.lag_days|f1]] d. Its global maximum, however, is at zero lag ([[e25.ccf_zero_lag|f3]] against [[e25.ccf_peak|f3]]), and the positive-lag peak sits at the window's edge. Around the global peak the centroid is [[e25.post_hoc.B_global_peak_centroid.lag_days|f1]] ± [[e25.post_hoc.B_global_peak_centroid.boot_err|f1]] d: correlated variations, no resolved lag.

![AGN variability against controls](figs/v2/e25_agn.svg "a, excess variance of the [[e25.n_agn]] AGN over [[e25.n_control]] matched controls in each wavelength bin, in units of its error; horizontal bars are the bin widths and dashed lines mark ±3σ. b, stacked correlation between the 0.75–2.4 µm and 2.4–5 µm light curves against lag (positive when the longer wavelengths lag) for the AGN, for all controls, and for random sets of [[e25.n_agn]] controls (1–99% band); the dotted line is the 99th percentile of the peak for time-shuffled AGN light curves.")

**Asteroid-size model.** Thermal sizes from SPHEREx 3–5 µm came out [[f3.median_ratio|f2]] times the NEOWISE sizes for the first [[f3.n]] asteroids. A recalibration of the thermal beaming parameter was designed but its independent test set was not completed, so the model was withdrawn.

**Distant-body search.** It has been tested only on bodies planted into the pictures, not on a real distant object, so it is not used for results. At distance $d$ a body's yearly motion is dominated by parallax $\varpi = 1\,\mathrm{au}/d$, so its track is the Section 5 model with a small drift $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ for orbital motion. The code inverts the geometry exactly: for each trial distance it solves $\lvert \mathbf{E} + \rho\,\hat{\mathbf{u}} \rvert = d$ for the geocentric range $\rho$ along the apparent direction $\hat{\mathbf{u}}$, with $\mathbf{E}$ the Earth's position. We searched [[e21.area_deg2|f3]] deg² at the north ecliptic pole on detector 1, reusing our earlier distant-body search code unchanged. [[e21.n_visits]] visits were chosen farthest-first in time over [[e21.baseline_days|f0]] days. Sources are detected with a Gaussian matched filter at 6.5σ, and detections recurring within one pixel in three or more visits are removed as static. The rest are linked over 40 trial distances from 30 to 2,000 au. A candidate needs $\chi^2/\nu \le 4$, a parallax of at least 5σ, at least half the visits, members spanning a quarter of the baseline, and a parallax–drift correlation below 0.98, without which distance and drift cannot be separated.

In [[e21.n_null_runs]] searches with each visit given another visit's time, [[e21.n_null_false]] produce a candidate, so the false-alarm rate is below [[e21.null_false_rate_upper95|pct]] per search (95% upper limit). The real search finds [[e21.n_candidates]] candidates. Bodies planted into copies of every visit's pixels, [[e21.injection_per_cell]] per cell of a per-visit S/N × distance grid, pass through the whole search. A 12σ-per-visit body at 500 au is recovered at [[e21.check500_fit_au|f1]] ± [[e21.check500_fit_err_au|f1]] au. At 500 au half of the planted bodies are recovered from [[e21.smallest_radius_half_recovered_rearth.radius|f0]] Earth radii (reflected light, albedo 0.3). The best cell, [[e21.best_cell_k]] of [[e21.best_cell_n]], supports a 95% exclusion only for bodies of at least [[e21.smallest_radius_95_rearth.radius|f0]] Earth radii at 1,200 au. Below about 400 au the parallax loop exceeds the stamp and recovery is near zero. The field is too small, and single pictures too shallow, to exclude a planet-sized body.

![Completeness of the distant-body search](figs/v2/e21_completeness.svg "Fraction of injected distant bodies recovered against heliocentric distance, for seven per-visit signal-to-noise ratios (48 injections per point). Bars are 68% Wilson intervals; the dotted line marks half recovered.")

**South-ecliptic-pole distant bodies.** The south-pole field had [[e21_sep.n_visits]] visits over only [[e21_sep.baseline_days|f0]] days, too short to separate parallax from drift. The 500 au check body was detected in most visits but could not be fitted, and the best cell recovered [[e21_sep.best_cell_k]] of [[e21_sep.best_cell_n]]. This field needs a second SPHEREx season.

**RW Aurigae.** The first test found no change meeting its rule in two detectors. A second statistic, chosen after seeing per-visit values from the same pictures, exceeds every comparison star ($Q$ = [[e28s.rwaur_Q|f1]], threshold [[e28s.rwaur_Q_threshold|f1]], comparison maximum [[e28s.rwaur_comparison_Q_max|f1]]): brightening at 1–2 µm ([[e28s.rwaur_D1_contrast|pct]], [[e28s.rwaur_D2_contrast|pct]]) and fading at 4–5 µm ([[e28s.rwaur_D5_contrast|pct]], [[e28s.rwaur_D6_contrast|pct]]). It is not blind and the binary is blended: a lead for the next season, not a result.

## 11. Prior work and what is new {#prior}

**SPHEREx.** Bock et al. (2026) describe the mission and survey; Akeson et al. (2026) the pipeline, its ePSF, flags and planned variability products. SPHEREx spectra of brown dwarfs are already published at scale: SPIFF (Gagné et al. 2026), 37 L0–Y4 dwarfs including WISE 0855−0714 (Rustamkulov et al. 2026), and young L/T dwarfs (Kiman et al. 2026). Our brown-dwarf spectra repeat known measurements with a per-picture fit at the moving position; we add a methane rule with leave-one-out accuracy, and a parallax. SPIFF does not model parallax, calling it negligible at SPHEREx's pixel scale, and we found no published parallax from SPHEREx positions. References come from Gaia DR3 (Gaia Collaboration, Vallenari et al. 2023), Luhman & Esplin (2016) and Luhman et al. (2024).

**Methods from other fields.** The cotrending follows SysRem (Tamuz, Mazeh & Zucker 2005) and Kepler's cotrending-basis-vector correction (Stumpe et al. 2012; Smith et al. 2012). The anomaly scorers are the unsupervised random forest (Baron & Poznanski 2017) and the isolation forest (Liu, Ting & Zhou 2008). Their calibration uses conformal prediction (Vovk, Gammerman & Shafer 2005) as conformal outlier $p$-values (Bates et al. 2023). The detector follows real–bogus classifiers for the Dark Energy Survey (Goldstein et al. 2015) and the Zwicky Transient Facility (Duev et al. 2019), and template-free detection (Acero-Cuellar et al. 2023). Shift-and-stack follows KBMOD (Whidden et al. 2019). Stacking along known orbits is established practice with NEOWISE and is not claimed as new, and the ePSF is that of Anderson & King (2000).

**What is new** is applying these methods to SPHEREx flight data, where every picture's changing wavelength is the central difficulty, and calibrating them on that data: false-alarm rates from fields, stars or time orderings not used for tuning, depths from injected sources, and significances from empty paths. We found no published learned change detector, light-curve cotrending or unsupervised anomaly search on SPHEREx flight data (search of 2026-11-15).

